☕ Days Minus-2 to 0: Foundations, OOP Mechanics, and Language Core
Focus on the object lifecycle, language keywords, exception frameworks, and foundational I/O mechanics.
Day 1: OOP Deep Dive & Language Keywords
  • Object Lifecycle & Creation: What happens under the hood when you use the new keyword? Compare this with object creation via reflection, cloning (Cloneable), and deserialization.
  • Access Modifiers & Encapsulation: Detail the precise visibility boundaries of private, default (package-private), protected, and public. When should you favor composition over inheritance?
  • Polymorphism Mechanics: Explain the difference between compile-time (overloading) and runtime (overriding) polymorphism. How does the JVM use virtual method tables (Vtables) to resolve overridden methods?
  • Keywords (final, static, abstract):
	• What are the architectural differences between an abstract class and an interface (especially post-Java 8/9 with default and private methods)?
	• What does final mean when applied to a class, a method, and a variable?
	• How do static blocks, static variables, and static methods behave during class loading?
Day 2: Exception Handling, Strings, and Memory Basics
  • The Exception Hierarchy: Differentiate between Throwable, Error, Exception (Checked), and RuntimeException (Unchecked). What is the operational cost of throwing an exception (stack trace generation)?
  • Robust Resource Management: Compare classic try-catch-finally blocks with Java 7+ try-with-resources. What interface must a class implement to be used in try-with-resources (AutoCloseable), and how are suppressed exceptions handled?
  • String Architecture & The Pool: Why are String objects immutable in Java? Explain the inner workings of the String Constant Pool (SCP) and how String.intern() works.
  • String Manipulation Performance: Directly compare String, StringBuilder, and StringBuffer. In what scenarios does the compiler automatically optimize string concatenation, and when does it introduce memory overhead?
  • Pass-by-Value: Prove why Java is strictly pass-by-value and never pass-by-reference, specifically illustrating what happens when you pass an object reference into a method.
Day 3: Core Java I/O, Serialization, and Functional Foundations
  • Java I/O Fundamentals: Differentiate between Byte Streams (InputStream/OutputStream) and Character Streams (Reader/Writer). How does a BufferedReader optimize I/O compared to an unbuffered stream?
  • Serialization Mechanics: How do you make a class serializable? What is the role of serialVersionUID, and what happens if there is a mismatch during deserialization? How does it interact with transient fields?
  • Java 8 Functional Interfaces: Master the core built-in functional interfaces: Predicate, Function, Consumer, and Supplier. What makes an interface a @FunctionalInterface?
  • Lambda Expressions & Method References: How do lambda expressions map to functional interfaces? What is target typing, and how do lambdas handle variable capturing (the "effectively final" constraint)?
  • The Optional Container: Why was Optional introduced in Java 8? What are the anti-patterns of using Optional (e.g., using it as method parameters or class fields), and how does it prevent NullPointerException?
